[Music]  welcome back so in this example video in  this jupiter notebook i want to walk you  through one of the cautionary tales kind  of one of the pitfalls that you can fall  into when you're doing the SVD the  singular value decomposition and again  this example is from our book  data-driven science and engineering so  you can go download the code and play  around with this yourself but the SVD is  particularly good when your data is  aligned in a way that individual rows  mean the same thing across all of your  columns okay so let's let's talk about  some examples of this so if I'm doing  the eigenfaces example where I have a  bunch of human faces and I'm reshaping  these into X vectors X 1 and X 2 then  this eigenfaces example only really  works when these these faces are kind of  cropped and aligned in the same way  across every face so that if I'm  comparing I'm gonna take the remember  the SVD is based on the inner product or  the core of the correlation matrix or  the inner product matrix of all of these  these images with each other and so when  I'm taking the inner product of these  two images I want to make sure that the  pixel that corresponds to an eye here  corresponds to an eye here so what we  need is for that row to mean the same  thing kind of an apples to apples  comparison across all of the columns if  I have an example where one faces up in  the left corner and one faces down in  the bottom right corner so that you know  nothing lines up then this is gonna be  really a kind of useless decomposition  it's not gonna help us understand the  structure of human faces unless things  are cropped and aligned in the right way  similarly we looked at that ovarian  cancer data sets and in that ovarian  cancer data set we had 216 patients each  of them had 4,000 genetic markers and  this is a great example of data that's  aligned because it was the exact same  4,000 genetic markers across every  single individual person and so in this  case looking at correlations across  these columns in these rows really is an  apples to  apples comparison from patient to  patient okay but if I take a simple  system that I know is low rank and I  just do something simple like rotated or  in this case translate it it destroys  that correlation structure in the data  okay so this is a very simple example to  illustrate how bad things can get you  can play around with it yourself  oftentimes we use the SVD in physical  systems where the columns of X are  measurements of a physical system as  they evolve in time like maybe I have  some traveling wave structure that I'm  measuring in time and even though our  human eyes are really good and our human  brains are good at recognizing patterns  like translational invariants and  rotational invariance the SVD can't  figure that out so it's a big caution  that if you have data that is  translating or rotating the SVD is not  going to give you a good representation  until you kind of factor out that  translation and rotation in the case of  images that means cropping and aligning  okay so here in this two paterno book  we're going to cook up an example that  is a really simple rank one system here  we have this this matrix this is  essentially an image of a matrix of  zeros and ones just white and black and  so you can tell that this is a very very  low rank matrix in fact this matrix  would be the product of this column  times that row okay that's what this  matrix is it's a rank one matrix just as  zeros and ones and if I take the SVD of  this all find that there is exactly one  dominant mode and everything else is  tiny at the machine precision but if I  do something silly like I rotate this  matrix even a little bit so I rotate  this matrix by by 10 degrees now when I  take the SVD of this matrix it's no  longer aligned there's this rotation we  as humans know that there's this  rotational invariance it's still one  object it's a square it's kind of a rank  one object in my mind but I compute the  SVD of this I'm gonna have an explosion  of the rank these singular values are  not going to decay I'm gonna have a lot  of modes I need to keep track of  and so plotting the singular values here  this is the the singular value spectrum  of the properly aligned square that is  in fact ranked 1 and you can see there's  one mode and then a bunch of stuff at  the noise floor this is 10 to the minus  12  but in my rotated matrix spectrum you  see that there is this explosion of rank  I need 250 modes to capture just the  same square but rotated by a few degrees  ok so this just points out the danger of  working your SVD on data that might be  rotating or translating ok so even  though we know about those in variances  the SVD doesn't and it causes a big  problem in this rank explosion so we can  also do this kind of exaggerated case  where we take the square and we rotate  through lots of angles and what we can  see here is that it's a little faint but  you can see it goes from kind of read  through dark blue through light blue and  as you go through those different  progressions of angles you can see this  rank getting worse and worse and worse  and worse you get this explosion of rank  as you rotate this out of alignment ok  to us it's still a rank 1 object but to  the the SVD and Python it doesn't know  about that ok and this is actually one  of the major successes of neural  networks at least is translation  invariance in these convolutional neural  network so that's a good reason people  use these so often because CNN's can  actually handle a lot of this this  translation of objects and pictures  rotations are still pretty tricky other  symmetries are tricky but I just want to  point out this cautionary tale that when  you use the SVD for data that's rotating  or translating you're gonna run it in  trouble ok thank you

